5. Step-by-Step Packet Tracer Execution (As Demonstrated in Video)

Step 1: Place Devices

1.  Drag 2 Cisco 2911 Routers onto the canvas.
2.  Drag 2 Generic PCs (PC0 on the left, PC1 on the right).

Step 2: Add Serial Cards (Critical Physical Step!)

By default, the 2911 router does not have serial ports.

1.  Click Router 0 \rightarrow go to the Physical tab.
2.  Click the router's Power Switch to turn it OFF (green light goes dark).
3.  From the left modules list, select HWIC-2T (high-speed serial card).
4.  Drag the module into an empty expansion slot on the router.
5.  Turn the Power Switch back ON.
6.  Repeat the exact same steps for Router 1.

Step 3: Cable the Topology

1.  Select Copper Straight-Through Cable (solid black line):
      - Connect PC0 (FastEthernet0) \rightarrow R0 (GigabitEthernet0/0).
      - Connect PC1 (FastEthernet0) \rightarrow R1 (GigabitEthernet0/0).
2.  Select Serial DCE Cable (red line with clock icon):
      - Click R0 first \rightarrow select Serial0/3/0 (first click makes R0 the
        DCE side).
      - Click R1 \rightarrow select Serial0/3/0 (this becomes DTE side).

Step 4: Configure the PCs

  - PC0: Desktop tab \rightarrow IP Configuration \rightarrow IP: 192.168.1.10,
    Mask: 255.255.255.0, Gateway: 192.168.1.1.
  - PC1: Desktop tab \rightarrow IP Configuration \rightarrow IP: 192.168.2.10,
    Mask: 255.255.255.0, Gateway: 192.168.2.1.

Step 5: Configure Host Table & RIP Routing (As Shown in Video)

In the video, the instructor adds host mappings and enables RIP so the routers
exchange routes and ping works end-to-end:

On R0:

R0# configure terminal
R0(config)# ip host R1 10.0.0.2
R0(config)# ip host PC1 192.168.2.10
R0(config)# router rip
R0(config-router)# version 2
R0(config-router)# network 192.168.1.0
R0(config-router)# network 10.0.0.0
R0(config-router)# exit
R0(config)# end
R0# copy running-config startup-config

On R1:

R1# configure terminal
R1(config)# ip host R0 10.0.0.1
R1(config)# ip host PC0 192.168.1.10
R1(config)# router rip
R1(config-router)# version 2
R1(config-router)# network 192.168.2.0
R1(config-router)# network 10.0.0.0
R1(config-router)# exit
R1(config)# end
R1# copy running-config startup-config

Step 6: Test Connectivity

1.  Use the Add Simple PDU envelope tool (or PC command prompt).
2.  Ping from PC0 to PC1.
3.  Note: The first ping may say Failed (due to ARP resolution and RIP table
    convergence). Click the Fast Forward Time button (>>) and send the packet
    again \rightarrow it will show Successful in the bottom-right status window.

6. Common Pitfalls & Viva Questions Highlighted in the Video

1.  Why does an interface show "Status: Up, Protocol: Down"?
      - Status (Layer 1 - Physical): The cable is plugged in and no shutdown has
        been executed.
      - Protocol (Layer 2 - Data Link): Down because either the device on the
        other side is powered off, the serial link is missing the clock rate, or
        encapsulation formats mismatch (e.g., HDLC vs PPP). Both must say up /
        up for traffic to pass.
2.  Why does only one side of a Serial link get clock rate?
      - In telecom/WAN links, the carrier/telco side is the DCE (Data
        Communications Equipment), which provides synchronization pulses. The
        customer router is the DTE (Data Terminal Equipment). In a lab
        simulation with a back-to-back serial cable, one router must act as DCE
        and generate this clock signal (clock rate 64000), otherwise the link
        will never negotiate Layer 2 protocol state.
3.  What is Configuration Register 0x2102 in show version?
      - It tells the router to boot normally: read the Cisco IOS from Flash and
        load the configuration from NVRAM.
